# DO THIS FIRST {-}

Copy and paste this into your R Console to start positpal, but replace `FILENAME.Rmd` with the name you are going to use for the .R or .Rmd file you are going to upload (for this challenge, rename the template first, e.g. `Group_#_Midterm.Rmd`, and use that exact name). **Do not include it as a code chunk.**

```
install.packages("remotes")
remotes::install_url("https://quantitative.bio/downloads/positpal.tar.gz", upgrade = "never")
positpal::start("FILENAME.Rmd")
```

Before you run it: open the project folder for this challenge in Positron (*File → Open Folder…*) so that it is your working directory, save this file in that folder under the name you will upload, and check that autosave is on (*File → Auto Save*). The first time you use positpal on a computer, `start()` will ask you to run `positpal::accept_eula()` once; do that, then run the `start()` line again. After a few minutes of work, check in the Explorer pane that the `.capture/snaps` folder inside your project is filling up with files. If it is not, or if `start()` refuses to record, run `positpal::doctor("FILENAME.Rmd")` and bring the output to tutorial or office hours. Run the `start()` line again at the start of every session you spend on this file.

# Part 1 — Importing and basic data inspection

The four files are in the `data` folder: `sample_metadata.csv`, `otu_table.csv`, `taxonomy_table.csv` and `alpha_metrics.csv`. The instructions describe each file and its columns.

- In `sample_metadata.csv`, each row is one sample record. `Sample_full` identifies individual records, while `Sample_AC` identifies samples shared across replicates. Other columns describe location, year, sample type, DNA concentration (`Stock_Conc`) and the total number of sequencing reads for the sample.
- In `otu_table.csv`, each row is one sample and each ASV column contains sequencing read counts. The first column (the sample name) has no header, so R will call it `X`.
- In `taxonomy_table.csv`, each row is one ASV, identified by `ASVID`, with taxonomic assignments from `Kingdom` to `Species`.
- In `alpha_metrics.csv`, each row is one sample, with its `Shannon` and `Simpson` diversity metrics.

## 1. Load packages

Load the packages you will use (Chapter 10, *Setup*): `dplyr`, `tidyr`, `tibble` and `ggplot2` (or `tidyverse`, which loads all of them).

```{r load-packages, message=FALSE}
library(tidyverse)

```

## 2. Import the four files

Import each file and save them as `otu`, `tax`, `metadata` and `alpha_metrics`. For `tax`, the row names should be the `ASVID` column.

Hint: refer to Chapter 3, *Import Data* in RCC2e.

```{r import-data}
#otu <-
otu <- read.csv(
  "/Users/coraliespakowski/Desktop/BIOL 343/BIOL343_Midterm/Data/otu_table.csv"
)

#tax <-
tax <- read.csv(
  "/Users/coraliespakowski/Desktop/BIOL 343/BIOL343_Midterm/Data/taxonomy_table.csv"
)

#metadata <-
metadata <- read.csv(
  "/Users/coraliespakowski/Desktop/BIOL 343/BIOL343_Midterm/Data/sample_metadata.csv"
)

#alpha_metrics <-
alpha_metrics <- read.csv(
  "/Users/coraliespakowski/Desktop/BIOL 343/BIOL343_Midterm/Data/alpha_metrics.csv"
)
```

## 3. Inspect the data and data cleaning

Inspect `otu`, `tax`, `metadata` and `alpha_metrics` using `str()` (for `otu`, which has over 2,000 columns, use `dim()` and `str()` on the first few columns only, e.g. `otu[, 1:5]`), then clean the objects:

- Identify the `ASVID` values where `Order` is `Chloroplast` in `tax`. Remove the corresponding rows from `tax` and columns from `otu`, saving the results as `tax_cleaned` and `otu_cleaned`. Watch out: many ASVs have no `Order` (`NA`), and a comparison with `NA` is `NA`, not `FALSE`.
- Use `rownames_to_column()` (from the `tibble` package) to move the ASV row names in `tax_cleaned` into a column called `ASVID`.
- Filter out the rows of `metadata` with `NA` in the `Type` or `total_reads` columns (this also removes the negative-control records), saving the result as `metadata_filtered`.
- Remove the replicate sample records from `metadata_filtered` using `distinct()` on `Sample_AC`.

**Do not edit the original files by hand**; make all changes in R.

Hint: refer to **R Basics → Data Types**, **R Basics → Objects & Variables**, **Data Science → 2D Data Wrangling** and **Data Science → Naughty Data** in RCC2e.

```{r inspect-and-clean}
#str()
dim(otu)
str(otu[, 1:5])
str(tax)
str(metadata)
str(alpha_metrics)

#identify the chloroplast ASVs
#chloroplast_asv <-
chloroplast_asv <- tax %>%
  filter(Order == "Chloroplast") %>%
  select(ASVID)
#dataframe to vector
chloroplast_asv <- as.vector(chloroplast_asv$ASVID)

#remove the identified ASVs from both tax and otu
#otu_cleaned <-
otu_cleaned <- otu %>%
  select(-any_of(chloroplast_asv))
#tax_cleaned <-
tax_cleaned <- tax %>%
  select(-any_of(chloroplast_asv))

#filter NAs from Type and total_reads and remove the replicate samples using Sample_AC
#metadata_filtered <-
metadata_filtered <- metadata %>%
  filter(!is.na(Type), !is.na(total_reads)) %>%
  distinct(Sample_AC, .keep_all = TRUE)

```

## 4. Joining the files

Combine `metadata_filtered` and `alpha_metrics` to create a new object called `alpha_merged`. Retain `Sample_AC`, `Type`, `Location`, `Whole_or_dissected`, `Shannon` and `Simpson`.

Hint: **Data Science → Join Datasets and 2D Data Wrangling** in RCC2e.

```{r join-alpha}
#alpha_merged <-
alpha_merged <- left_join(alpha_metrics, metadata_filtered)
alpha_merged <- alpha_merged %>%
  select(Sample_AC, Type, Location, Whole_or_dissected, Shannon, Simpson)
```

# Part 2 — Preparing and visualizing the data

## 5. Visualizing total read counts

Use `ggplot()` to visualize the distribution of `total_reads` across the samples in `metadata_filtered`. Select an appropriate `binwidth` (or number of `bins`) for the histogram. Give the bars a fill colour and a border colour, and label both axes.

Hint: refer to **Quick Visualizations → Histogram**, **Basic Customization → fill and colour (or color)** and **Basic Customization → lab, xlab, and ylab and Themes and Geoms** in RCC2e.

```{r histogram-total-reads}
# histogram plotting the distribution of total reads across samples
ggplot(metadata_filtered, aes(x = total_reads)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 30000, colour = "pink", fill = "maroon") +
  labs(x = "Total reads across samples", y = "Frequency")
```

## 6. Prepare the ASV data for plotting

Using `otu_cleaned`:

- Convert the OTU table from wide format to long format using `pivot_longer()`.
- Store the ASV identifiers in a column called `ASVID` and the read counts in a column called `Reads`.
- Rename the column `X` (the unnamed sample column) to `Sample_AC`.
- Join with `metadata_filtered` using `Sample_AC`.
- Group the data by `Sample_AC`, `Type`, `Location` and `Whole_or_dissected`.
- Remove samples where the total number of reads is 0.
- Calculate the *relative abundance* of each ASV within each sample.
- As a final step, join the taxonomy table (`tax_cleaned`) by `ASVID`.
- Save the resulting object as `otu_long`.

Relative abundance is the proportion of a sample's reads that belong to one ASV, a number between 0 and 1 (see the instructions, *Relative abundance*); the relative abundances of all ASVs in a sample add up to 1:

$$
\text{Relative abundance} =
\frac{\text{Reads of microbe A in sample 1}}
{\text{Total reads of all microbes in sample 1}}
$$

For our data, create a new column `Relative_abundance` and calculate it by `Reads / sum(Reads)` within each sample.

*Note: use the pipe operator (`%>%`) to do all of this in one step instead of creating multiple new objects.*

Hint: refer to **Data Science → Wide vs Long Data**, **Data Science → Join Datasets** and **Data Science → 2D Data Wrangling** in RCC2e.

```{r prepare-otu-long}
# otu_long <-
otu_long <- otu_cleaned %>%
  pivot_longer(
    cols = ASV1:ASV2053,
  ) %>%
  rename("Sample_AC" = "X", "ASVID" = "name", "Reads" = "value") %>%
  full_join(metadata_filtered, by = "Sample_AC") %>%
  group_by(Sample_AC, Type, Location, Whole_or_dissected) %>%
  filter(Reads != 0) %>%
  mutate(Reads/sum(Reads))
full_join(tax_cleaned, by = "ASVID")
```

## 7. Visualizing the top 7 phyla

Use `otu_long` to:

- Calculate the relative abundance of each bacterial phylum within each sample.
- Identify the 7 most abundant phyla across all samples.
- Group all remaining phyla (including ASVs with no phylum) into an "Other" category.
- Create a stacked bar plot showing phylum relative abundance for each sample.
- Make two versions of the plot: one that uses `facet_wrap()` to separate the samples by `Location`, and one that separates them by `Type`.
- Include appropriate axis labels, a legend title, and `theme_classic()`.
- Rotate the sample labels on the x-axis so they are easier to read.

Hint: refer to **Data Science → 2D Data Wrangling** in RCC2e and, to create the stacked bar plot, **Quick Visualizations → Basic Graphs**, **Basic Customization → labs, Themes and Geoms**, **Multi-plot Graphs → facets** and the **ggplot2 cheatsheet** in OnQ. *Use `geom_col()` to create the stacked bar plot instead of `geom_bar()`.*

```{r phylum-plots}

```

